diff --git a/docs/manual/impairments.md b/docs/manual/impairments.md
index 4960d7b..12b02d6 100644
--- a/docs/manual/impairments.md
+++ b/docs/manual/impairments.md
@@ -16,5 +16,49 @@ Labs are meant to be a reflection of real-world scenarios. To make simulated net
 These commands allow users to set, show and reset link impairments (delay, jitter, packet loss) on any link that belongs to a container node and create labs simulating real-world network conditions.

 ```bash title="setting packet loss at 10% rate on eth1 interface of clab-netem-r1 node"
 containerlab tools netem set -n clab-netem-r1 -i eth1 --loss 10
 ```
+
+## Impairments in the topology
+
+Impairments can also be part of the topology, so they are set whenever the lab is deployed. The `netem` key of a link endpoint takes the same impairments as `tools netem set`, which apply to the packets the endpoint's interface sends:
+
+```yaml
+topology:
+  links:
+    - type: veth
+      endpoints:
+        - node: r1
+          interface: eth1
+          netem:
+            delay: 50ms
+            jitter: 5ms
+            loss: 0.5
+        - node: r2
+          interface: eth1
+          netem:
+            rate: 10000
+```
+
+| Key          | Meaning                                                               |
+| ------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `delay`      | Time to delay the packets, e.g. `100ms`                               |
+| `jitter`     | Delay variation, e.g. `10ms`; needs a `delay`, at most `2.147483647s` |
+| `loss`       | Random packet loss in percent                                         |
+| `rate`       | Rate limit in kbit/s                                                  |
+| `corruption` | Random packet corruption in percent                                   |
+
+Brief links take a list with the impairment of each endpoint, in the order of the endpoints. An empty entry leaves its endpoint unimpaired:
+
+```yaml
+topology:
+  links:
+    - endpoints: ["r1:eth1", "r2:eth1"]
+      netem:
+        - delay: 50ms
+        - {}
+```
+
+Impairments are supported on the node endpoints of veth links; other link types and `host` or `mgmt-net` endpoints reject them. Lab runtimes such as [clabernetes](clabernetes/runtime.md) don't apply them yet, so deploying a topology with impairments there fails.
+
+Containerlab sets them again when a node is started or restarted, since a node's interfaces lose their impairments when it is stopped, and when the lab is reconciled with a changed topology. `tools netem` can still change them while the lab runs, until containerlab sets the topology's impairments again. Removing an impairment from the topology doesn't remove it from a running lab; use `tools netem reset` for that.
